David Damerell wrote:

> But this is just reducing the racket to what I always suspect it is; not a
> way to ascend a particular character, but a way to shoot craps until one
> gets lucky.

That's somewhat unfair. There's an -awful- lot more than luck
involved in a protection dive, although it plays a factor. By
those standards, the whole -game- is a craps shoot until a
character gets "lucky".

The racket is, IMO, a valid strategy. My success rate at
performing the racket has increased considerably with practice;
and, more importantly, my survival rate has increased a -lot-.
(My failure rate and death rate have a pretty comfortable margin
between them, although I haven't kept close count. I'd say
perhaps half die during the dive, but somewhere from a tenth to
a fifth actually succeed, depending on race and role.)

(I don't run big n' dumb types through the racket, generally,
so these rates are just from the more traditional racket classes.)

Also note that I usually play SLASH'EM, in which a racket
failure can neatly be segued into a power boost of some sort
at the Mall. As a side note, necromancers are -extremely- well-
suited to the racket, thanks largely to their starting ghoul; I
succeed with something like a third of 'em, and rarely die trying.

